    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense for the basic fields, but I'm also worried about how this will affect future discovery if we just use \"unknown\" for everything. Is there a more nuanced way to use controlled vocabularies or specific metadata extensions to capture *why* the attribution is missing? Like, if it's a matter of the signature being worn away vs. there being no signature at all, should those be treated differently in the schema?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To encapsulate the nuanced reasons behind unknown creator attribution, use the Dublin Core's 'dcterms-relation' and 'dc:contributor' properties in conjunction with the controlled vocabularies provided by the Library of Congress or other relevant metadata initiatives (like ArMA, Facets). For instance, 'dcterms-relation': 'isProvidedBy' can help to indicate the link between the map and its unknown creator, while 'dc:contributor': 'creatorName' helps differentiate cases where a name might traditionally be expected from one where it is literally absent. Additionally, a custom metadata element that includes a note explaining the absence of attribution—possibly using ARIA (Accessible Rich Internet Applications) standards—can be created and integrated within the Dublin Core Level Metadata set. These custom extensions should be accompanied by standardized but detailed narratives within the PREMIS (Preservation Metadata: Implementation Strategies) standards to inform future archivists and preserve the context of why creators were uncertain or unidentifiable.\n\nIn this way, the implementation can achieve granular levels of complexity that differentiate between various states of missing creator information. Here's how it might look:\n\n1. If a creator's name or signature cannot be discerned due to the map being worn:\n   -",
